['Here's a simple C code snippet that implements the atoi() function:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int my_atoi(char* str) {
    int result = 0;
    int sign = 1;

    if (*str == '-') {
        sign = -1;
        str++;
    } else if (*str == '+') {
        str++;
    }

    while (*str != '\0') {
        if (*str >= '0' && *str <= '9') {
            result = result * 10 + (int)(*str - '0');
        } else {
            break;
        }
        str++;
    }

    return result * sign;
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    int i;

    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s <number>\n", argv[0]);
        exit(1);
    }

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        printf("argv[%d] = `%s'\n", i, argv[i]);
    }

    int number = my_atoi(argv[1]);

    printf("atoi() result: %d\n", number);

    return 0;
}
```

This program first checks if the command line argument is valid. If not, it prints an error message and exits. Then it uses the `my_atoi()` function to convert the string to an integer and prints the result.

The `my_atoi()` function skips any leading '+' or '-' signs, then reads characters from left to right until it encounters a character that's not a digit. It returns the resulting integer value.']

