            return render_template_string('<html><body>You are logged in, but you are not an admin. Please go to the user page at {{ url_for("user") }} or <a href="{{ url_for("index") }}">log out</a>.</body></html>')
        else:
            return redirect(url_for('admin'))

# user route
@app.route('/user')
def user():
    if 'logged_in' not in session:
        return render_template_string('<html><body>You are not logged in. Please <a href="{{ url_for("index") }}">log in</a>!</body></html>')
    else:
        return 'Welcome to the user page!'

# admin route
@app.route('/admin')
def admin():
    if 'logged_in' and 'admin' not in session:
        return render_template_string('<html><body>You are logged in, but you are not an admin. Please go back to the main page at {{ url_for("index") }}.</body></html>')
    else:
        return 'Welcome to the admin page!'

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.secret_key = 'supersecretkey'
    app.run(debug=True)
```

This code creates a Flask application with three routes: `/`, `/login`, and `/user`. When you run this script, it will automatically start the development server for your Flask application.

The `@app.route('/')` function is used to define the root route of our application. This route returns 'Welcome to the main page!' as the response when you visit the root URL.

For the login route (`/login`), if a user tries to access this route without being logged in, they will be redirected to the index page with a message telling them that they are not logged in and need to log in. If they are logged in but not an admin, they will also be sent back to the index page with a message about their login status.

The `/user` route returns 'Welcome to the user page!' as the response when you visit this URL. However, if a user tries to access this route without being logged in, they will be redirected to the index page with an error message.

Similarly, the admin route (`/admin`) also checks for login and admin status before allowing the user to view the admin page.']

