4. When a newline character is found, we break out of the loop and null-terminate the buffer.
5. We allocate memory for a new string using `malloc` and copy the contents of the buffer to it using `strcpy`.
6. The function returns the pointer to the new string.

Note that this implementation assumes that the input string will not exceed 1023 characters (since the buffer is only 1024 bytes). If you need to handle longer strings, you'll need to increase the size of the buffer or use a different approach."]

